<section class="py-20 px-6 bg-stone-light">
  <div class="max-w-[900px] mx-auto reveal">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-display font-bold text-ink tracking-tight mb-8 text-center">
      What Is Ancient Chinese Architecture?
    </h2>

    <div class="prose prose-lg prose-stone max-w-none text-ink-muted space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Ancient Chinese architecture is one of the world's oldest continuous building traditions, spanning over
        <strong class="text-ink">3,000 years</strong> from the Shang dynasty (1600 BCE) through the fall of
        the Qing in 1911. Unlike the stone monuments of Egypt or Greece, Chinese builders perfected
        <strong class="text-ink">timber-frame construction</strong> — a system of interlocking wooden columns,
        beams, and brackets that proved remarkably resilient against earthquakes while allowing for wide,
        graceful roofs with deep overhanging eaves.
      </p>

      <p>
        At its heart lies the <strong class="text-ink">dougong</strong> (斗拱), an intricate bracket system
        that distributes roof weight onto columns without a single nail. This engineering marvel, combined
        with a strict devotion to <strong class="text-ink">axial symmetry</strong>, glazed ceramic roof tiles
        in imperial yellow and temple green, and courtyard layouts that reflect Confucian social hierarchy,
        created an architectural language instantly recognizable across East Asia. Chinese designs directly
        shaped the temple architecture of Japan, the palaces of Korea, and the pagodas of Vietnam.
      </p>

      <p>
        Though wood's vulnerability to fire and decay means few buildings older than the Tang dynasty (618–907)
        survive above ground, masterpieces like the <strong class="text-ink">Forbidden City</strong>,
        <strong class="text-ink">Temple of Heaven</strong>, and the remote Tang-era
        <strong class="text-ink">Foguang Temple</strong> still stand — and remarkably,
        <strong class="text-ink">many are open to visitors</strong>. Whether you are planning a trip,
        researching for a project, or simply curious about the roof you saw in a photograph, everything
        on this site is built to help you explore, understand, and experience China's architectural heritage.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 pt-8 border-t border-stone-dark/30 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-6 text-sm text-ink-muted">
